@node Exceptions
@nodedescription Raising and handling exceptions.
@section Exceptions
This section describes Scheme's exception-handling and exception-raising
procedures.  For the concept of Scheme exceptions, @pxref{Error
situations and unspecified behavior}.  @xref{Exception handling} for the
@code{guard} syntax.

@cindex exception handlers
@define{Exception handlers} are one-argument procedures that determine
the action the program takes when an exceptional situation is signaled.
The system implicitly maintains a current exception handler in the
dynamic environment.

@cindex current exception handler
The program raises an exception by invoking the current exception
handler, passing it an object encapsulating information about the
exception.  Any procedure accepting one argument can serve as an
exception handler and any object can be used to represent an exception.

@deffn procedure with-exception-handler handler thunk

It is an error if @var{handler} does not accept one argument.  It is
also an error if @var{thunk} does not accept zero arguments.

The @nc{with-exception-handler} procedure returns the results of
invoking @var{thunk}.  @var{Handler} is installed as the current
exception handler in the dynamic environment used for the invocation of
@var{thunk}.

@ifinfo
@lisp
(call-with-current-continuation
 (lambda (k)
  (with-exception-handler
   (lambda (x)
    (display "condition: ")
    (write x)
    (newline)
    (k 'exception))
   (lambda ()
    (+ 1 (raise 'an-error))))))
                               @output{condition: an-error}
                               @ok{exception}

(with-exception-handler
 (lambda (x)
  (display "something went wrong\n"))
 (lambda ()
  (+ 1 (raise 'an-error))))
                               @output{something went wrong}
@end lisp
@end ifinfo
@ifnotinfo
@lisp
(call-with-current-continuation
 (lambda (k)
  (with-exception-handler
   (lambda (x)
    (display "condition: ")
    (write x)
    (newline)
    (k 'exception))
   (lambda ()
    (+ 1 (raise 'an-error))))))
                               @ok{exception}
           @andprints{condition: an-error}

(with-exception-handler
 (lambda (x)
  (display "something went wrong\n"))
 (lambda ()
  (+ 1 (raise 'an-error))))
           @prints{something went wrong}
@end lisp
@end ifnotinfo

After printing, the second example then raises another exception.
@end deffn

@deffn procedure raise obj

Raises an exception by invoking the current exception handler on
@var{obj}.  The handler is called with the same dynamic environment as
that of the call to @nc{raise}, except that the current exception
handler is the one that was in place when the handler being called was
installed.  If the handler returns, a secondary exception is raised in
the same dynamic environment as the handler.  The relationship between
@var{obj} and the object raised by the secondary exception is
unspecified.
@end deffn

@cindex continuable exception
@deffn procedure raise-continuable obj

Raises an exception by invoking the current exception handler on
@var{obj}.  The handler is called with the same dynamic environment as
the call to @nc{raise-continuable}, except that: (1) the current
exception handler is the one that was in place when the handler being
called was installed, and (2) if the handler being called returns, then
it will again become the current exception handler.  If the handler
returns, the values it returns become the values returned by the call to
@nc{raise-continuable}.

@ifinfo
@lisp
(with-exception-handler
  (lambda (con)
    (cond
      ((string? con)
       (display con))
      (else
       (display "a warning has been issued")))
    42)
  (lambda ()
    (+ (raise-continuable "should be a number")
       23)))
                              @output{should be a number}
                              @ok{65}
@end lisp
@end ifinfo
@ifnotinfo
@lisp
(with-exception-handler
  (lambda (con)
    (cond
      ((string? con)
       (display con))
      (else
       (display "a warning has been issued")))
    42)
  (lambda ()
    (+ (raise-continuable "should be a number")
       23)))
    @prints{should be a number}
                              @ok{65}
@end lisp
@end ifnotinfo
@end deffn

@deffn procedure error message obj @value{defdots}

@var{Message} should be a string.

Raises an exception as if by calling @nc{raise} on a newly allocated
implementation-defined object which encapsulates the information
provided by @var{message}, as well as any @var{obj}s, known as the
@define{irritants}.  The procedure @nc{error-object?}  must return
@nc{#t} on such objects.

@lisp
(define (null-list? l)
  (cond ((pair? l) #f)
        ((null? l) #t)
        (else
          (error
            "null-list?: argument out of domain"
            l))))
@end lisp
@end deffn

@deffn procedure error-object? obj

Returns @nc{#t} if @var{obj} is an object created by @nc{error} or one
of an implementation-defined set of objects.  Otherwise, it returns
@nc{#f}.  The objects used to signal errors, including those which
satisfy the predicates @nc{file-error?} and @nc{read-error?}, may or may
not satisfy @nc{error-object?}.
@end deffn

@deffn procedure error-object-message error-object

Returns the message encapsulated by @var{error-object}.
@end deffn

@deffn procedure error-object-irritants error-object

Returns a list of the irritants encapsulated by @var{error-object}.
@end deffn

@deffn procedure read-error? obj
@deffnx procedure file-error? obj

Error type predicates.  Returns @nc{#t} if @var{obj} is an object raised
by the @nc{read} procedure or by the inability to open an input or
output port on a file, respectively.  Otherwise, it returns @nc{#f}.
@end deffn
